<Base
  title="Pool Green But Pump Is Running? Here's Why"
  description="A running pump moves water — it doesn't sanitize it. The real causes of a green pool with working circulation, in check order. Call (512) 764-9867."
>
  <h1>Why is my pool green even though the pump runs?</h1>

  <p>
    A pump that runs fine only proves water is moving — it says nothing about whether that water
    is sanitized. Green pools with a healthy pump usually come down to one of four things:
    chlorine demand outpacing supply, stabilizer (CYA) buildup locking chlorine out, poor
    filtration despite circulation, or a phosphate load feeding algae faster than chlorine can
    keep up.
  </p>

  <Dispatch problem="Pool green despite pump running fine" area={SITE.serviceArea} />

  <h2>The myth: circulation equals sanitation</h2>
  <p>
    Circulation and sanitation are two separate jobs. The pump's job is to move water through
    the filter and past the point where chlorine gets introduced. Whether that chlorine actually
    kills algae once it's there depends on chemistry — pH, free chlorine level, and stabilizer —
    none of which the pump controls. A pump can run perfectly for weeks while the water
    underneath it slowly loses the chemical fight.
  </p>

  <h2>Check in this order</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Free chlorine level.</strong> Test it directly rather than assuming your feeder or
      chlorinator is dosing correctly. A reading near zero despite regular chemical additions
      points toward demand or lockout, not a dosing problem.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stabilizer (CYA).</strong> High CYA from years of trichlor tabs chemically
      handcuffs chlorine so it can't sanitize even at a normal-looking reading. This is common
      enough in Central Texas pools that it deserves its own look — see our guide on{' '}
      <a href="/guides/pool-shock-not-working-cya/">why shocking a pool sometimes fails outright</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Filter condition and actual run time.</strong> A pump that runs on a timer doesn't
      guarantee the filter is doing its job — high filter pressure, a torn cartridge, or a
      multiport valve stuck between settings can all let water bypass real filtration while the
      pump hums along normally.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Phosphate load.</strong> Fertilizer runoff, decaying leaves, and some tap water
      sources introduce phosphates that feed algae directly. In a phosphate-heavy pool, chlorine
      is fighting a food source it can't remove on its own.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why this trips people up</h2>
  <p>
    It's intuitive to assume a green pool means broken equipment, because a broken pump is the
    most visible possible failure. But we run far more service calls where the pump is fine and
    the chemistry is the actual problem — which is also good news, because chemistry problems are
    almost always cheaper and faster to fix than equipment failures.
  </p>

  <h2>When to call a pro</h2>
  <p>
    Basic testing — chlorine, pH, and a CYA reading if your kit includes one — is safe and
    worth doing before you spend on chemicals. Call in a technician once you're past guessing:
    if CYA is high enough to need a partial drain, if the filter needs inspection you can't do
    yourself, or if the pool has already gone fully green and you want an honest recovery
    timeline instead of trial and error. Our{' '}
    <a href="/guides/neglected-green-pool-recovery/">green-to-clean recovery guide</a> covers
    what that process actually looks like.
  </p>

  <section class="faq">
    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <h3>Could my pump be running but not actually filtering?</h3>
    <p>
      Yes — a stuck multiport valve, a torn cartridge, or a badly clogged filter can all let the
      pump run normally while filtration is compromised. High filter pressure compared to a
      clean-filter baseline is usually the first clue.
    </p>
    <h3>If chlorine reads normal, can the pool still be green from something else?</h3>
    <p>
      It can, though it's less common. Phosphate load and early-stage algae that hasn't yet
      consumed the day's chlorine dose can both produce a green tint even with a chlorine reading
      that looks acceptable at the moment you test it.
    </p>
    <h3>Is a green pool with a running pump more or less urgent than one with a dead pump?</h3>
    <p>
      Roughly the same — a dead pump makes things worse faster, but a green pool with working
      circulation isn't fixing itself either. Both are worth addressing before a Central Texas
      summer week turns it from cloudy to fully green.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Dispatch problem="Pool green despite pump running fine" area={SITE.serviceArea} />
</Base>
